<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Business flow "artio-fix": the AMPS instance the Artio FIX engine publishes
  into.

  What arrives here is RAW FIX, byte for byte as it came off the session:
  SOH-separated tag=value, never re-encoded. That is the whole point of the
  bridge - Artio hands the library a DirectBuffer, the bridge copies it into a
  byte array and calls the AMPS byte array publish, and no String, no JSON and
  no schema exists anywhere on that path. AMPS parses FIX natively
  (MessageType fix), so SOW keys and content filters name FIX TAG NUMBERS:
  /11 is ClOrdID, /17 ExecID, /37 OrderID.

  The client selects that parser in its connection URI, not per topic:

      tcp://127.0.0.1:9007/amps/fix

  Connect on /amps/json to these topics and nothing parses; you get a
  connection and then silence, which is a confusing failure. Every publisher
  and every reader of this instance uses /amps/fix.

  TOPIC MAP, and the one rule that shapes it
  ==========================================
  Publishing to a SOW topic a message that does not carry the topic's KEY
  FIELD is silent, unbounded data loss. So the topic set is not "one topic per
  consumer taste" - it is driven by which tag is guaranteed present on which
  FIX message type:

      35=D / G / F  (NewOrderSingle, CancelReplace, Cancel)  always carry 11
      35=8          (ExecutionReport)                        always carry 17 and 37
      session admin (35=0 / 1 / 2 / 4 / 5 / A / 3)           carry no order tag at all

  which is exactly the split below.

  Be precise about what "silent" means here, because it is worse than the
  usual telling. It is widely said that AMPS REJECTS a SOW publish lacking its
  key. Measured against 5.3.5.135 with MessageType fix, it does not: the
  message is accepted, no error reaches the publisher, nothing is logged, and
  the record is stored under one degenerate key shared by every such message.
  Publish three keyless messages and the topic holds ONE record: the third.
  The first two are gone, and the only visible symptom is a SOW that looks
  oddly short.

  The bridge's TopicRouter enforcing "this tag must be present before routing
  here" (see docs/03) is therefore not a belt-and-braces nicety mirroring a
  server check. It is the ONLY thing standing between a mis-tagged message and
  a quietly truncated blotter.

  Ports: 9007 amps/tcp, 9008 websocket, 8085 admin UI.
-->
<AMPSConfig>

    <Name>artio-fix</Name>

    <!--
      Admin/monitoring HTTP interface: http://localhost:8085/

      SQLTransport names a <Transport> below, NOT a port. It turns on the SQL
      console in the admin UI, which is the fastest way to look at what the
      bridge published without writing a client: the page is served over HTTP
      on 8085 but runs its queries over the websocket transport, so a browser
      needs to reach 9008 as well.
    -->
    <Admin>
        <InetAddr>8085</InetAddr>
        <SQLTransport>amps-websocket</SQLTransport>
        <FileName>./stats/stats.db</FileName>
        <Interval>10s</Interval>
    </Admin>

    <Transports>
        <!-- One transport serves every message type; the client picks the type
             in its URI path. The bridge and the test harness both use
             /amps/fix on this one. -->
        <Transport>
            <Name>amps-tcp</Name>
            <Type>tcp</Type>
            <InetAddr>9007</InetAddr>
            <ReuseAddr>true</ReuseAddr>
            <Protocol>amps</Protocol>
        </Transport>

        <!-- Same instance over WebSocket: browser clients and the admin UI's
             SQL console. Required by <SQLTransport> above. -->
        <Transport>
            <Name>amps-websocket</Name>
            <Type>tcp</Type>
            <InetAddr>9008</InetAddr>
            <ReuseAddr>true</ReuseAddr>
            <Protocol>websocket</Protocol>
        </Transport>
    </Transports>

    <!--
      stdout, so `podman logs` is the log. Both the shell script's `wait` and
      the test harness detect readiness by watching for the server's own
      startup-complete line (message code 00-0015) there; route logging to a
      file instead and readiness detection goes blind.

      Note for anyone editing the comments in this file: AMPS ECHOES THE WHOLE
      CONFIG into that same log at startup, comments included. So a comment
      here that quoted the startup-complete line verbatim would appear in the
      log about a second BEFORE the server was actually up, and every reader
      watching for it would be fooled. That is why the phrase is described
      rather than quoted, and why the readiness marker both consumers match on
      includes the numeric message code.
    -->
    <Logging>
        <Target>
            <Protocol>stdout</Protocol>
            <Level>info</Level>
        </Target>
    </Logging>

    <!--
      ===================================================================
      SOW - State of the World
      ===================================================================
      Declaring a topic here turns a stream into a queryable, keyed table
      maintained by the server: sow queries, sow_and_subscribe, delta_publish,
      OOF and per-record expiration all become available for it. A topic NOT
      declared here still works - it is an ordinary dynamic pub/sub topic,
      created on first publish, with no state kept. fix.raw and fix.admin are
      deliberately of that second kind.

      <FileName> is spelled out for every topic rather than using %n. %n
      expands to the topic name, and these names contain dots; the expansion
      would work today but the explicit filename is what makes `ls
      amps-server/data/artio-fix/sow` readable, and it survives a topic rename
      that would otherwise silently orphan the old file.

      EXPECT THIS WARNING at every startup, once per topic below:

        00-0007 Topic 'fix.orders' has regular expression characters which
        could cause unintentional misrouting of messages.

      It is the dot. AMPS notices that a <Name> could be read as a pattern and
      says so. It is noise here, and was checked rather than assumed:
      publishing to fixZorders on 5.3.5.135 does NOT land in fix.orders (the
      SOW query returns nothing, and fixZorders is not a topic at all), so the
      names route literally. The dotted names come from the plan and are the
      names the bridge and every consumer use; do not rename them to silence
      the warning. Note that the same is NOT true of <Pattern>, which really
      is a regex.
    -->
    <SOW>

        <!--
          fix.orders - one record per ClOrdID.

          Keyed /11 because ClOrdID is the client's id for THIS request and is
          mandatory on 35=D, 35=G and 35=F. A cancel/replace carries a new 11
          and the previous one in 41, so this topic holds one record per
          REQUEST, not per order chain: three records for an order that was
          placed, replaced and cancelled. That is the audit shape, and it is
          the one that survives out-of-order delivery.

          JOURNALLED. It is a system of record: the requests this engine sent
          or received, as sent. Nothing derives it.
        -->
        <Topic>
            <Name>fix.orders</Name>
            <MessageType>fix</MessageType>
            <Key>/11</Key>
            <FileName>./sow/fix-orders.sow</FileName>
            <Durability>persistent</Durability>
        </Topic>

        <!--
          fix.execs - one record per ExecID: the fills history.

          Keyed /17 because ExecID is unique per ExecutionReport, so this is a
          keyed event log rather than a last-value store: every 35=8 keeps its
          own record. "How did this order fill" is a filtered query here
          (/37 = 'the OrderID'), ordered by whatever the reports carry.

          JOURNALLED. Same reason as fix.orders: it is the record of what the
          counterparty actually said, and fix.order.state is derived from it.
        -->
        <Topic>
            <Name>fix.execs</Name>
            <MessageType>fix</MessageType>
            <Key>/17</Key>
            <FileName>./sow/fix-execs.sow</FileName>
            <Durability>persistent</Durability>
        </Topic>

        <!--
          fix.order.state - one record per OrderID: the live blotter.

          Keyed /37 (OrderID, the venue's id for the order), and fed only
          35=8. FIX execution reports are CUMULATIVE SNAPSHOTS - each one
          carries the running 14 CumQty, 151 LeavesQty, 6 AvgPx and 39
          OrdStatus - so "the latest report per OrderID" IS current order
          state, with no aggregation anywhere.

          Requests (35=D/G/F) must never be published here: they carry no tag
          37, and rather than being refused they would all pile onto one
          keyless record, each overwriting the last. See the header.

          NOT journalled. Every record in it is the last message of a stream
          that fix.execs already journals, so replaying fix.execs rebuilds
          this topic exactly. Journalling it would write the same bytes twice.
        -->
        <Topic>
            <Name>fix.order.state</Name>
            <MessageType>fix</MessageType>
            <Key>/37</Key>
            <FileName>./sow/fix-order-state.sow</FileName>
            <Durability>persistent</Durability>
        </Topic>

    </SOW>

    <!--
      ===================================================================
      Topics with no SOW, listed here for the reader
      ===================================================================
      Neither of these needs a declaration: an AMPS topic that is not in <SOW>
      is created on first publish and keeps no state. They are described here
      because they are part of the flow's contract even though the file has
      nothing to say to the server about them.

      fix.raw - EVERY application message the engine saw, in arrival order,
          exactly as received. No SOW: a raw FIX message has no field that is
          guaranteed unique across message types, so there is no honest key,
          and a wrong key silently collapses records. Its consumers want
          history, not state.

          JOURNALLED (below). This is the tape. A bookmark subscription from
          Client.Bookmarks.EPOCH replays it from the beginning, which is how
          the harness counts what the journal holds and how a downstream
          rebuild starts from nothing.

      fix.admin - session-level traffic (Logon, Logout, Heartbeat, TestRequest,
          ResendRequest, SequenceReset, Reject) when the bridge is configured
          to publish it; by default it is not. No SOW and NOT journalled: it
          is operational noise for a live session monitor, it carries no order
          tag to key on, and Artio's own log is the durable record of the
          session. Journalling a heartbeat every 30 seconds per session would
          be the largest stream in the instance and the least useful.
    -->

    <!--
      ===================================================================
      TransactionLog
      ===================================================================
      The journal is the durable, ordered record of every message published to
      the topics listed here. It backs bookmark subscriptions ("replay me
      everything since X"), publisher deduplication, and SOW recovery after an
      unclean shutdown. A topic absent from this list is not replayable, at
      all - that is the choice being made for fix.order.state and fix.admin
      above, and the reason is written next to each.

      MinJournalSize 10MB is the FLOOR, not a tuning choice: ask for less and
      the server silently resets it to 10MB with a warning at startup
      (verified against 5.3.5.135). With PreallocatedJournalFiles 2 that means
      this instance reserves about 20MB of journal on first start, which is
      why amps-server/data is gitignored.

      Every entry names topic AND message type; `fix` here, matching the
      publishers.
    -->
    <TransactionLog>
        <JournalDirectory>./journal</JournalDirectory>
        <PreallocatedJournalFiles>2</PreallocatedJournalFiles>
        <MinJournalSize>10MB</MinJournalSize>

        <Topic>
            <Name>fix.raw</Name>
            <MessageType>fix</MessageType>
        </Topic>
        <Topic>
            <Name>fix.orders</Name>
            <MessageType>fix</MessageType>
        </Topic>
        <Topic>
            <Name>fix.execs</Name>
            <MessageType>fix</MessageType>
        </Topic>
    </TransactionLog>

</AMPSConfig>
